#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:4
msgid "Integration with LLM Engine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:6
msgid ""
"XGrammar enables efficient structured generation. In this tutorial, we go"
" over the key components of XGrammar and how to integrate XGrammar into "
"an LLM engine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:9
msgid ""
"We first lay out the concepts in :ref:`High-Level Flow <how-to-engine-"
"integration-flow>`. We then demonstrate how XGrammar enables "
":ref:`Structured Generation for Batched Inference <how-to-engine-"
"integration-batched>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:13
msgid ""
"The code snippets below are actual runnable code as we simulate the LLM "
"generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:26
msgid "High-Level Flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:28
msgid ""
"In this section, we go over the key components of XGrammar when "
"integrating it into an LLM engine for structured generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:41
msgid "xgr.TokenizerInfo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:43
msgid ""
"``xgr.TokenizerInfo`` is a per-model construct that encapsulates "
"tokenizer information, including all its vocabulary. There are several "
"ways of instantiating it, and the most convenient way is using an "
"``AutoTokenizer``. Note that for some models, ``AutoConfig.vocab_size`` "
"can be larger than ``AutoTokenizer.vocab_size`` due to paddings, with the"
" former being the shape of the model's logits. To be safe, always pass in"
" the former when instantiating ``xgr.TokenizerInfo``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:61
msgid "xgr.GrammarCompiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:63
msgid ""
"With an ``xgr.TokenizerInfo``, we can instantiate an "
"``xgr.GrammarCompiler``. This is a construct that compiles a grammar "
"according to the model's tokenizer info. Therefore, for each model, you "
"can use the same ``xgr.GrammarCompiler`` persistently, as it can compile "
"different grammars for the same ``xgr.TokenizerInfo``. Note that the "
"``compiler`` behavior can be configured with ``max_threads`` for "
"multithreading, and ``enable_cache`` (defaults to true) for caching "
"compiled grammars."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:76
msgid "xgr.CompiledGrammar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:78
msgid ""
"Then, using the ``xgr.GrammarCompiler``, we can compile a grammar, with "
"the result being an ``xgr.CompiledGrammar``. Here we use a built-in JSON "
"grammar. For other grammars, see :ref:`how-to-json-generation` and :ref"
":`how-to-ebnf-generation`. Every thing we have seen up to now are per-"
"model (rather than per-generation)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:88
msgid "xgr.GrammarMatcher"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:90
msgid ""
"With the compiled grammar, we can instantiate a ``xgr.GrammarMatcher``. "
"It is the main construct an LLM engine interacts with that maintains the "
"state of the structured generation. Note that each request should have "
"its own ``xgr.GrammarMatcher`` since each has a different generation "
"state, as we will see in :ref:`how-to-engine-integration-batched`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:101
msgid "Bitmasking Logits in Auto-regressive Generation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:103
msgid ""
"Now we simulate a single-request auto-regressive generation. See later "
"section for :ref:`how-to-engine-integration-batched`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:106
msgid ""
"First, we pre-allocate a token bitmask with "
"``xgr.allocate_token_bitmask()``, which is essentially a ``torch.Tensor``"
" of shape ``(batch_size, vocab_size)``. You can also use your own "
"implementation for allocating a bitmask."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:110
msgid ""
"In each auto-regressive step, we fill the token bitmask according to the "
"current state of the matcher with "
"``xgr.GrammarMatcher.fill_next_token_bitmask()``. Then, we apply the "
"bitmask into the model's logits with "
"``xgr.apply_token_bitmask_inplace()``, which calls a CUDA kernel if "
"``logits`` is on CUDA (recommended), otherwise a CPU implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:115
msgid ""
"After masking, the logits for illegal tokens are set to negative "
"infinity, so that we will never sample them. After sampling the token, "
"update the ``xgr.GrammarMatcher``'s state with "
"``xgr.GrammarMatcher.accept_token()``. Finally, use  "
"``xgr.GrammarMatcher.reset()`` to prepare for the next generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:160
msgid "Structured Generation for Batched Inference"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:162
msgid ""
"The code snippets above assume a single request generation. This section "
"demonstrates how the same concept works with batched generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:165
msgid ""
"First, follow the exact same steps above for the per-model constructs "
"``xgr.TokenizerInfo`` and ``xgr.GrammarCompiler``. Say each request needs"
" to generate a valid JSON."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:188
msgid ""
"Now, we need to maintain an ``xgr.GrammarMatcher`` for each request in "
"the batch, since each has a different generation state. Note that each "
"request in the batch can follow a different ``xgr.CompiledGrammar``, but "
"here for simplicity, they are all just following the general JSON "
"grammar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/engine_integration.rst:202
msgid ""
"We simulate an auto-regressive generation of batched inference. Note that"
" here we assume the generation lengths of the two requests are the same "
"for simplicity. But it should be easy to generalize based on how your "
"engine supports batched inference. The key difference from single-request"
" generation is that, in batched-request generation, each request has its "
"own ``xgr.GrammarMatcher`` to maintain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/json_generation.rst:4
msgid "JSON Generation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/json_generation.rst:6
msgid ""
"XGrammar enables efficient structured generation. One example structure "
"is JSON and JSON Schema. In this tutorial, we go over how to use XGrammar"
" to ensure that an LLM's output is a valid JSON, or adheres to a "
"customized JSON schema."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/json_generation.rst:10
msgid ""
"We first go over how to use XGrammar in an LLM engine to achieve this in "
":ref:`JSON Generation in LLM Engines <how-to-json-generation-engine>`, we"
" then provide an end-to-end JSON generation using XGrammar with HF "
"``transformers`` in :ref:`Try out via HF Transformers <how-to-json-"
"generation-HF>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/json_generation.rst:25
msgid "JSON Generation in LLM Engines"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/json_generation.rst:27
msgid ""
"In this section, we see how to use XGrammar in an LLM engine to ensure "
"that the output is always a valid JSON."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/json_generation.rst:57
msgid ""
"For JSON generation, there are generally three options for compiling the "
"grammar: using a built-in JSON grammar, specify JSON schema with a "
"Pydantic model, or from a JSON schema string. Pick one one of the three "
"below to run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/portable_api.rst:4
msgid "Portable API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/portable_api.rst:6
msgid ""
"XGrammar is implemented with a lightweight C++ core that can be "
"integrated into many platforms. Besides the C++ backend, we also provide "
"ready-to-use Python and JavaScript/TypeScript libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/portable_api.rst:9
msgid ""
"For the Python library, simply check out the :ref:`Python API Reference "
"<apixgrammar>`. Below we take a high-level view of the Javascript "
"library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/portable_api.rst:15
msgid "Javascript SDK for Web-based LLMs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/portable_api.rst:17
msgid ""
"The JS SDK is designed to be used for LLMs that run in the browser, "
"including `WebLLM <https://github.com/mlc-ai/web-llm>`__. WebLLM "
"integrated with XGrammar's JS SDK, ``web-xgrammar``. It uses `emscripten "
"<https://emscripten.org/>`__ to compile the C++ code into WebAssembly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how_to/portable_api.rst:22
msgid ""
"To use this SDK, simply run ``npm install @mlc-ai/web-xgrammar``. For "
"more, see `here <https://github.com/mlc-ai/xgrammar/tree/main/web>`__."
msgstr ""

